## Getting started (Dev Container)

This project ships with a VS Code dev container so the entire toolchain
(Python, `uv`, Flask, Postgres) runs inside Docker.

#### Prerequisites

1. [Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/)
2. [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/) with the
   [Dev Containers extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-vscode-remote.remote-containers)
3. [just](https://just.systems/) (optional — only needed if you run recipes from the host)

#### Setup

1. Open this folder in VS Code and choose **Reopen in Container** when prompted.
2. Once the container is built, in the integrated terminal run:

       just init

3. Start the dev server:

       just serve

The app is served on http://localhost:5050.

> ⚠️ **Do not run `uv`, `flask`, or `pytest` from your host shell.** The IDE
> terminal is attached to the `web` container — run all Python tooling there.
> Running these on the host bypasses the Docker environment and will produce
> inconsistent results (missing dependencies, no DB connectivity, etc.).
{%- else -%}
## Getting started

All Python tooling runs inside the `web` container. The `just` recipes wrap
each command with `docker compose` so you don't need `uv`, Flask, or pytest
installed on your host.

#### Prerequisites

1. [Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/)
2. [just](https://just.systems/)

#### Setup

    just init
    just serve

The app is served on http://localhost:5050.

> ⚠️ **Do not run `uv`, `flask`, or `pytest` directly on your host.** Every
> recipe in the `justfile` routes through `docker compose exec web ...` for a
> reason — running these tools on the host bypasses the container's database
> and dependencies and will produce inconsistent results. If a recipe is
> missing, add a new one rather than running the underlying command on the host.
{%- endif %}

## Common tasks

| Command                  | What it does                              |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| `just init`              | Initial project setup                     |
| `just serve`             | Start the Flask dev server on `:5050`     |
| `just migrate "<msg>"`   | Generate a new database migration         |
| `just db-upgrade`        | Apply pending migrations                  |
| `just db-downgrade`      | Roll back the last migration              |
| `just test`              | Run the test suite                        |
| `just logs`              | Tail logs from the `web` container        |
| `just upgrade`           | Bump and re-sync dependencies             |
